=== plain-80: width 80, height 24, profile notty, dark, glyphs ascii, plain
# play engine/refused-d9-ubuntu2604 ----------------------------$
$
# [i] Note$
# Play mode: hardn's own screens and prompts, on the real$
# plan/apply engine against a simulated host, hostfake's$
# recording host.System, whose commands a model of the host$
# answers. Nothing on this machine changes: every command and$
# file is the fake's, and its record is shown when play ends.$
$
# [i] Info: engine/refused-d9-ubuntu2604$
# web-01.example.net runs Ubuntu 26.04, hardened by hardn$
# v0.3.2: its manage.conf, and a listen.conf for port 2230$
# written with no daemon-reload, so ssh.socket still listens on$
# 22.$
# The change: hardn's v0.3.2 SSH files moved into 00-hardn.conf.$
# The plan refuses it, with a report (P-39 (a)).$
$
# [!] Operation Failed$
# /etc/systemd/system/ssh.socket.d/listen.conf (hardn v0.3.x)$
# sets ssh.socket to port 2230, but it never took effect:$
# ssh.socket is configured for [[::]:22 0.0.0.0:22] (no daemon-$
# reload since it was written, D9). Migrating it now could not$
# be rolled back: the rollback's daemon-reload would move$
# ssh.socket to port 2230 alone, off the port this host is$
# reached on (P-39) (SSH_LISTEN_CONF_NOT_IN_EFFECT)$
$
  [i] Info: Check the log file at /var/log/hardn.log for details$
  [i] Info: To go on: first make sure the firewall lets port$
      2230 through: ssh.socket then listens on port 2230 alone.$
      Then let listen.conf take effect while this session stays$
      open: run `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl restart$
      ssh.socket` (established SSH sessions survive the$
      restart), check that a new SSH connection to port 2230$
      works, and run hardn again. A reboot also lets it take$
      effect, but ends this session: reboot only with console$
      access, in case port 2230 is not reachable.$
$
# simulated system ---------------------------------------------$
$
# [i] Note$
# Nothing was applied.$
$

